---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import TaxesModule from "../../components/TaxesModule.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import RegionNote from "../../components/RegionNote.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Taxes: The Drag & Asset Location"
  description="Tax on a taxable account is a leak that compounds — but you can blunt it. Watch the drag widen year by year and see where it comes from, then learn which account should hold your bonds versus your stocks."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>Keeping more of what you earn</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Tax isn't a one-off haircut on your returns — it's a leak that compounds, a
      little more every year. Two tools for blunting it: watch the gap between a
      sheltered and a taxable account widen and see exactly what causes it, then
      learn the free tax win of putting the right asset in the right account.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <RegionNote>
      US-focused — these tools assume US tax treatment: ordinary-income vs.
      long-term capital-gains rates, and US account types (Roth and Traditional
      IRAs / 401(k)s) alongside a taxable brokerage. The mechanisms exist wherever
      some accounts are taxed and others sheltered; the specific rates and accounts
      shown are American.
    </RegionNote>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <TaxesModule client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The leak, and how to blunt it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The drag compounds.</strong> Dividends and the gains a fund
          distributes are taxed the year they happen, even if you reinvest — money
          pulled out of the compounding machine forever, so the shortfall grows
          faster than the tax itself. High-turnover funds are the villain; deferral
          is the superpower.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Location is a free win.</strong> Asset <em>allocation</em> is how
          much you hold in stocks versus bonds; asset <em>location</em> is which
          account each one sits in. Same portfolio, same risk — but shelter the
          tax-hungry asset and you keep more of it. Traditional space usually wants
          the bonds; Roth space often wants the highest-returning asset instead.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Both keep the mechanism clear by simplifying — a single lump sum, no state
        taxes or tax-loss harvesting. Use them to see the logic, not as filing
        instructions. Educational only, not financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="taxes" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/taxes" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro { padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md); max-width: var(--measure-wide); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  .wl-explainer { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
</style>
